# ADR-deviation marker — harness gates the publish
- date: 2026-11-05
- branch: fix/harness-gates-publish
- adr_governing: ADR-004 (publish pipeline safety contract)
- outcome: ADR-deviation
- lens-a-supporting-adr: tony-hoare — ADR-004 pins an ordered step list as a
  contract, and adding a step changes it. The list is also already wrong
  (Node 20 vs 22; five steps missing), so the honest move is to say so rather
  than add a thirteenth line to a list that misdescribes twelve.
- lens-b-supporting-deviation: michael-nygard — every check in job 1 reads a
  file list; none ran the package. A tarball can pass the six-file floor and
  still fail to initialise, which the harness demonstrated on its first
  honest run. A contract that inspects but never executes is not protecting
  the thing it names.
- decision-rationale: The operator asked for the package to be tested before
  release. ADR-004's list is a pinned contract, so adding a step amends it.
  Three facts make amending right. An npm version can never be reused, so the
  answer is only free before the gate. The harness already existed and only
  ever ran after the event it judged. And its result gated nothing, so six
  failures went unread. Hoare's objection is answered by naming the drift in
  the amendment instead of papering over it — reconciling the full list is a
  separate change with its own review.
- new-adr-filed: none — amends ADR-004, which already carries an amendment
  from earlier today. Operator instruction 2026-11-05: "reorganize the
  sequence so package is tested before release."
